  "query_preview": "..\nSynoptic Setup: Forecast begins with a large ridge hot high \npressure running from the Intermountain West into western Montana \nand a Trough of cooler pacific air dropping down from northern \nBritish Columbia to off the coast of the Pacific Northwest. \nBetween these two features SW flow has setup with pacific moisture\nand minor disturbance ridding in from off the coast of Northern \nCalifornia to northern Idaho. \n\nToday through Friday: Ridge moving over the area will provide for\ndry conditions and warm temperatures with highs in 80s. As the\ntrough to the west approaches and high pressure exits east Friday\nthe Pressure gradient will lend to gusty winds Friday afternoon \nout of the S to SE. \n\nFriday night into Saturday night: This will last till the front \nmoves through that evening and switches the gusts to the west to\nnorthwest. There is some question on to whether the gusts will sty\nup overnight behind the front leading to lower confidence. A Lake\nWind Advisory has been issued from 8AM Friday morning 9AM Saturday\nmorning for these gusts. Also, expect cooler temps with highs int\nhe 60s from here onward. \n\nSunday through Monday: Current model data suggests a disturbance\nmoving through southern/southeastern Montana on entering SW flow \nwill generate some rain showers across the area as it passes \nthrough. An isolated thunderstorm may be possible during this \ntime. Currently the bulk of QPF is over the Yellowstone Valley of\nthe GGW CWA, but confidence is low as the track appears to be \nshifting SE and totals are lowering quickly with the last few \nforecasts. \n\nMonday night onward: Forecast in general looks to become semi- \nzonal with a minor trough embedded in it. However, ensembles are \nin disagreement with strength which is reducing confidence in the\nforecast from here onward.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nNo changes from NBM.",
